  "plain_language": "We irrigated a sandy desert soil every day with water of increasing saltiness. The twin shows how much salt builds up in the soil (mean 71.1 mmol/L at 8 dS/m) and that the soil microbes' hydrogen uptake falls 43.9 % compared with the unirrigated dry soil, mostly because the soil stays wet. How much a crop would suffer (e.g. alfalfa relative yield 0.887 at 8 dS/m; none of the crops loses yield below 4 dS/m here, because the twin's bucket leaches most of the salt) comes from published crop-salinity curves, not from the twin; the twin does not model plants.",
  "question": "What if we irrigate with brackish groundwater or treated wastewater?",
